meta {
  name: Refund an order
  type: http
  seq: 2
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/admin/orders/:id/refunds
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}

params:path {
  id: 1
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{superToken}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "amount": "10.00",
    "reason": "Damaged in transit",
    "externalReference": "RTN-1"
  }
}

docs {
  **Super admin only**, and checked twice: the route policy refuses a staff token, and the
  service checks again before the order is even loaded. This is the one operation that moves
  money out of the business, and a route is one mapping mistake away from being wrong.

  A refund **credits**, it does not edit. The invoice is untouched and a credit note is issued
  against it — accounting runs on documents that do not change once issued, and the linkage is
  the only way to tell a partial refund from an edited invoice.

  Over the refundable remainder is **422 `REFUND_EXCEEDS_REFUNDABLE`**, which tells you how much
  is left rather than merely refusing.
}
